icon":147},"Journal and deception","\u002Fsecurity\u002Fjournal","5.security\u002F5.journal","i-lucide-book-lock",{"title":149,"path":150,"stem":151,"icon":152},"Compliance and posture","\u002Fsecurity\u002Fcompliance","5.security\u002F6.compliance","i-lucide-scale",{"title":154,"path":155,"stem":156,"icon":109},"SIEM export","\u002Fsecurity\u002Fsiem-export","5.security\u002F7.siem-export",{"title":158,"path":159,"stem":160,"icon":43},"Wazuh and Suricata","\u002Fsecurity\u002Fwazuh-suricata","5.security\u002F8.wazuh-suricata",{"title":162,"icon":163,"path":164,"stem":165,"children":166,"page":54},"Reference","i-lucide-book-marked","\u002Freference","6.reference",[167,172,177,182,187],{"title":168,"path":169,"stem":170,"icon":171},"Agent protocol","\u002Freference\u002Fprotocol","6.reference\u002F1.protocol","i-lucide-arrow-left-right",{"title":173,"path":174,"stem":175,"icon":176},"UI API","\u002Freference\u002Fui-api","6.reference\u002F2.ui-api","i-lucide-braces",{"title":178,"path":179,"stem":180,"icon":181},"Metric storage","\u002Freference\u002Fmetric-storage","6.reference\u002F3.metric-storage","i-lucide-database",{"title":183,"path":184,"stem":185,"icon":186},"Performance","\u002Freference\u002Fperformance","6.reference\u002F4.performance","i-lucide-gauge",{"title":188,"path":189,"stem":190,"icon":191},"Development","\u002Freference\u002Fdevelopment","6.reference\u002F5.development","i-lucide-code",{"id":193,"title":10,"body":194,"description":409,"extension":410,"links":411,"meta":412,"navigation":413,"path":6,"seo":414,"stem":7,"__hash__":415},"docs_en\u002F1.getting-started\u002F1.index.md",{"type":195,"value":196,"toc":400},"minimark",[197,201,207,212,239,247,251,355,377,381,384,388],[198,199,200],"p",{},"Dodock Wolf is self-hosted observability for Frappe benches, ERPNext and Dokos sites and their\nLinux hosts. It catches 502 windows, failed nightly crons, worker crashes and suspicious logins\nbefore customers notice, and it keeps all data on a Frappe site you run.",[202,203],"screenshot",{"alt":204,"caption":205,"name":206},"The Wolf UI overview on the demo bench","The Wolf UI overview after the demo bench's 2AM scenario.","overview",[208,209,211],"h2",{"id":210},"two-parts","Two parts",[213,214,215,230],"card-group",{},[216,217,218],"card",{"icon":33,"title":30,"to":31},[198,219,220,221,225,226,229],{},"A Frappe app (",[222,223,224],"code",{},"dodock_wolf","). It stores events, metrics, rules and incidents as DocTypes,\nevaluates alert rules at each scheduler tick and shows the timeline in the Wolf UI at ",[222,227,228],{},"\u002Fwolf","\nand in Desk.",[216,231,232],{"icon":38,"title":35,"to":36},[198,233,234,235,238],{},"A Python daemon on each host, using only the standard library (Python 3.9 or later). It tails\nnginx, bench and auth logs, reads ",[222,236,237],{},"\u002Fproc",", Redis, MariaDB and TLS certificates, and pushes\nbatches over HTTPS.",[198,240,241,242,246],{},"The site running the Wolf App also watches itself, with no agent: failed scheduled jobs, Error\nLogs, failed logins, privileged role grants and risky changes such as new Server Scripts. Other\nsites on a bench can forward the same signals as ",[243,244,245],"a",{"href":41},"satellites",".",[208,248,250],{"id":249},"what-it-is-for","What it is for",[252,253,254,270],"table",{},[255,256,257],"thead",{},[258,259,260,264,267],"tr",{},[261,262,263],"th",{},"Signal",[261,265,266],{},"Example",[261,268,269],{},"Where it comes from",[271,272,273,289,303,317,334,344],"tbody",{},[258,274,275,279,286],{},[276,277,278],"td",{},"5xx and 502 spikes",[276,280,281,282,285],{},"14 × 502 on ",[222,283,284],{},"a.example.com"," in 5 min, after a deployment at 01:47",[276,287,288],{},"nginx access log (agent)",[258,290,291,294,297],{},[276,292,293],{},"Cron and scheduler failures",[276,295,296],{},"The nightly job failed at 02:00, or the scheduler stalled",[276,298,299,302],{},[222,300,301],{},"scheduler.log"," (agent), Scheduled Job Log (site)",[258,304,305,308,311],{},[276,306,307],{},"Worker problems",[276,309,310],{},"Worker OOM-killed, RQ queue backlog, no live worker",[276,312,313,316],{},[222,314,315],{},"worker.log",", Redis (agent)",[258,318,319,322,329],{},[276,320,321],{},"Host resources",[276,323,324,325,328],{},"Disk at 93 % on ",[222,326,327],{},"\u002F",", load per core above 2",[276,330,331,333],{},[222,332,237],{}," (agent)",[258,335,336,338,341],{},[276,337,121],{},[276,339,340],{},"SSH brute force, admin login from a new country, a web attack scan",[276,342,343],{},"auth log, nginx (agent); login and audit hooks (site)",[258,345,346,349,352],{},[276,347,348],{},"Operations",[276,350,351],{},"No backup for 26 h, TLS certificate expiring in 3 days, replication stopped",[276,353,354],{},"backups, TLS, MariaDB (agent)",[198,356,357,358,362,363,366,367,370,371,374,375,246],{},"Every signal becomes a ",[359,360,361],"strong",{},"Wolf Event"," or a ",[359,364,365],{},"Wolf Metric Sample",". ",[359,368,369],{},"Wolf Alert Rules"," turn them\ninto ",[359,372,373],{},"Wolf Incidents",", which notify your channels when they open, reopen and resolve. See\n",[243,376,18],{"href":19},[208,378,380],{"id":379},"what-it-is-not","What it is not",[198,382,383],{},"Dodock Wolf is not a full Datadog replacement. It does no LLM observability, data observability,\nreal-user monitoring or SLO dashboards. It covers operational safety for Frappe (errors, cron,\nworkers, hosts) and the security signals around it, using open formats (JSON logs, syslog,\nOCSF) and storage you own.",[208,385,387],{"id":386},"next","Next",[213,389,390,395],{},[216,391,392],{"icon":16,"title":13,"to":14},[198,393,394],{},"From nothing to a first alert, on one bench.",[216,396,397],{"icon":21,"title":18,"to":19},[198,398,399],{},"Events, metrics, rules, incidents and scopes.",{"title":401,"searchDepth":402,"depth":403,"links":404},"",1,2,[405,406,407,408],{"id":210,"depth":403,"text":211},{"id":249,"depth":403,"text":250},{"id":379,"depth":403,"text":380},{"id":386,"depth":403,"text":387},"What Dodock Wolf is, what it watches, and how its two parts fit together.","md",null,{},{"icon":11},{"title":10,"description":409},"aKjh6n1dev9OqRJBzpd1MVp8DKMwhyTvp-HMtw_eSho",[411,417],{"title":13,"path":14,"stem":15,"description":418,"icon":16},"Install the Wolf App, put an agent on the host and get a first alert, on one bench.",1795039392067]